{"content":[{"c":["MORNING STAR"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Literally, Venus. The phrase is closely related to the idea of the “dayspring” (",{"href":"?bref=Job.38.12","r":"Job.38.12","rawR":"Job.38.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.38.12","value":"Job.38.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jb 38:12"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Luke.1.78","r":"Luke.1.78","rawR":"Luke.1.78","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Luke.1.78","value":"Luke.1.78"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lk 1:78"},") and the “daystar” (",{"href":"?bref=2Pet.1.19","r":"2Pet.1.19","rawR":"2Pet.1.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Pet.1.19","value":"2Pet.1.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Pt 1:19"},"). The identity of the star is settled when Christ says, “I am the morning star” (",{"href":"?bref=Rev.22.16","r":"Rev.22.16","rawR":"Rev.22.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rev.22.16","value":"Rev.22.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Rv 22:16"},"). It is another way of saying, “I am the light of the world” (",{"href":"?bref=John.8.12","r":"John.8.12","rawR":"John.8.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.8.12","value":"John.8.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jn 8:12"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=John.9.5","r":"John.9.5","rawR":"John.9.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.9.5","value":"John.9.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"9:5"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=John.12.46","r":"John.12.46","rawR":"John.12.46","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.12.46","value":"John.12.46"}],"t":"ref","v":"12:46"},"). The central concept found in the symbol is that of Christ as light shining in darkness (",{"href":"?bref=Luke.2.32","r":"Luke.2.32","rawR":"Luke.2.32","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Luke.2.32","value":"Luke.2.32"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lk 2:32"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=John.1.4,7-9","r":"John.1.4,7-9","rawR":"John.1.4,7-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.1.4,7-9","value":"John.1.4,7-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jn 1:4, 7-9"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=John.3.19","r":"John.3.19","rawR":"John.3.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.3.19","value":"John.3.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:19"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=John.12.35","r":"John.12.35","rawR":"John.12.35","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.12.35","value":"John.12.35"}],"t":"ref","v":"12:35"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Cor.4.6","r":"2Cor.4.6","rawR":"2Cor.4.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Cor.4.6","value":"2Cor.4.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Cor 4:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Eph.5.14","r":"Eph.5.14","rawR":"Eph.5.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eph.5.14","value":"Eph.5.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Eph 5:14"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Pet.2.9","r":"1Pet.2.9","rawR":"1Pet.2.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Pet.2.9","value":"1Pet.2.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Pt 2:9"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Jn.2.8","r":"1Jn.2.8","rawR":"1Jn.2.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Jn.2.8","value":"1Jn.2.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Jn 2:8"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Rev.21.23","r":"Rev.21.23","rawR":"Rev.21.23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rev.21.23","value":"Rev.21.23"}],"t":"ref","v":"Rv 21:23"},"). With the birth of the Messiah, the morning star arose—the gospel light dawned (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.9.1-2","r":"Isa.9.1-2","rawR":"Isa.9.1-2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.9.1-2","value":"Isa.9.1-2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Is 9:1-2"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.4.15-16","r":"Matt.4.15-16","rawR":"Matt.4.15-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.4.15-16","value":"Matt.4.15-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mt 4:15-16"},"). The phrase points to Christ’s glory, as the source of light, and to his grace in the sharing of life. Christ not only described himself as the morning star but also declared that he gives the morning star to those who overcome (",{"href":"?bref=Rev.2.28","r":"Rev.2.28","rawR":"Rev.2.28","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rev.2.28","value":"Rev.2.28"}],"t":"ref","v":"Rv 2:28"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"MorningStar","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Jb 38:12","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Job.38.12","rawQueryValue":"Job.38.12","sourceRefExact":"Job.38.12"},{"anchorText":"Lk 1:78","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bre